"<strong>Altitude and azimuth.</strong> The simplest way to say where a star is: its altitude is its angle above the horizon ({#}° on the horizon, {#}° straight overhead), and its azimuth is its compass direction ({#}° north, {#}° east, {#}° south, {#}° west). Both change all night as Earth turns, and they are different for observers in different places.",
"<strong>Right ascension and declination.</strong> These are like longitude and latitude, but fixed on the sky. Declination is the angle north (+) or south (−) of the celestial equator, the line on the sky right above Earth’s equator. Right ascension is measured eastward around the sky in hours and minutes, from {#} h to {#} h. A star keeps the same right ascension and declination all night and all year (they change only very slowly, through precession), so star charts and catalogs use them.",
"<strong>Polaris and your latitude.</strong> Polaris lies within {#}° of the north celestial pole, the point in the sky right above Earth’s North Pole. Seen from latitude L north, the pole stands L degrees above the northern horizon. Ruston, Louisiana, is at latitude {#}° N.",
"<strong>Circumpolar stars.</strong> As the sky turns, every star circles the pole once a day. A star close enough to the pole never dips below the horizon: it is circumpolar. From latitude L north, a star is circumpolar if its declination is more than {#}° − L.",
"<strong>The ecliptic.</strong> As Earth orbits the Sun, the Sun appears to move eastward against the stars, once around the sky in a year, along a path called the ecliptic. The constellations it passes through are the constellations of the zodiac. The modern constellation boundaries were set by the International Astronomical Union in {#}.",
